Second-pass plan-space optimization (March 2026):

I reviewed the corrective-wave beads for user-facing quality, not just technical completeness.

Refinements made:
- tightened every new bead so completion explicitly requires focused unit coverage, integration coverage on the relevant shipped/library paths, and at least one deterministic e2e or scenario script with detailed replay-grade logging;
- made benchmark publication depend on shipped-path parity and zero-placeholder truth signals, so performance claims cannot outrun user-visible correctness;
- made unsupported-surface docs depend on parity, placeholder, advisory, and onboarding outputs so user-facing support contracts are generated from the same evidence the rollout system uses;
- added execution-tier migration expectations so renaming lane/tier concepts helps operators instead of confusing them.

The intent is to make the backlog harder to game and easier for future operators to trust.

