description:
File: crates/franken-engine/src/governance_context.rs:62-78 and crates/franken-engine/src/resource_certificate_consumer.rs:1092-1111
Pattern: RwLock-read-then-write
Code excerpt:
pub fn enforcer(&self) -> RwLockReadGuard<'_, BudgetEnforcer> { self.enforcer.read() }
pub fn enforcer_mut(&self) -> RwLockWriteGuard<'_, BudgetEnforcer> { self.enforcer.write() }
pub fn set_enforcer(&self, enforcer: BudgetEnforcer) { self.enforcer.replace(enforcer); }
...
pub fn replace(&self, enforcer: BudgetEnforcer) { *self.write() = enforcer; }
Why this is a bug: the API exposes raw read/write guards and also exposes replacement on `&self`. Rust permits a caller to hold `let guard = ctx.enforcer();` and then call `ctx.set_enforcer(new_enforcer)` in the same thread or callback path. `std::sync::RwLock` cannot upgrade a held read guard to a write guard, so this wedges the control-plane epoch/certificate rotation path instead of returning a busy/error result.
Repro / detection: add a stress/regression that holds `let _read = ctx.enforcer();` then calls `ctx.set_enforcer(...)` on the same context; the thread blocks on `SharedBudgetEnforcer::write()`. Static check: `rg -n "pub fn enforcer\(|pub fn enforcer_mut\(|pub fn set_enforcer\(|pub fn replace" crates/franken-engine/src/governance_context.rs crates/franken-engine/src/resource_certificate_consumer.rs`.
Suggested fix: avoid exposing raw guards on the composition root; provide closure-based inspection/mutation helpers that do not call back into `set_enforcer`, or make replacement use `try_write` and return a typed busy error when any read guard is active.

File: crates/franken-engine/src/control_plane_mock_inventory.rs:1116-1134,1542-1556,1616-1745,2365-2481,2674-2795,3000-3018
Pattern: lock-order-inversion
Code excerpt:
let lock_path = out_dir.join(".control_plane_mock_inventory.lock");
...
let lock_path = out_dir.join(".ambient_mock_guard.lock");
...
let lock_path = out_dir.join(".orchestrator_context_refactor.lock");
...
let repro_lock_path = out_dir.join("repro.lock");
write_*_atomic(&repro_lock_path, &repro_lock)?;
write_*_atomic(&run_manifest_path, &manifest_bytes)?;
Why this is a bug: three bundle writers use different per-workflow locks but publish into the same out_dir filenames (`repro.lock`, `run_manifest.json`, `events.jsonl`, `commands.txt`, `summary.md`, `env.json`, `trace_ids.json`). Concurrent workflows can each acquire their own lock and then overwrite shared final artifacts, producing a bundle with mixed provenance and stale manifest pointers.
Repro / detection: run write_control_plane_mock_inventory_bundle_in_root, write_ambient_mock_guard_bundle_in_root, and write_orchestrator_context_refactor_bundle_in_root concurrently with the same out_dir; final `repro.lock`/`run_manifest.json` is last-writer-wins while sibling artifacts may come from earlier writers. Static check: `rg -n "\.control_plane_mock_inventory\.lock|\.ambient_mock_guard\.lock|\.orchestrator_context_refactor\.lock|repro_lock_path = out_dir.join\(\"repro.lock\"\)" crates/franken-engine/src/control_plane_mock_inventory.rs`.
Suggested fix: use one canonical bundle lock per output directory for all control-plane mock inventory publication paths, or partition each workflow into a distinct subdirectory with non-overlapping artifact names.

